 "standard_url": "https://www.deped.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/MATATAG-Mathematics-CG-Grades1-4-and-7.pdf",
 "standard_text": "Distinguish a variable from a constant in an algebraic expression; evaluate algebraic expressions given the value/s of the variable/s; translate verbal phrases into algebraic expressions.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Grade 7 algebraic expressions worksheets for the DepEd MATATAG curriculum: variables and constants, translating verbal phrases, writing and evaluating expressions. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Variable: a letter that can change. Constant: a fixed number.",
 "key_sub": "Substitute with parentheses, then use GEMDAS.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>A <b>variable</b> is a letter that stands for a number that can change. A <b>constant</b> is a number whose value does not change.</li><li>In 4x − 7, 4 is the <b>coefficient</b> of x and −7 is the constant term.</li><li>Translate words carefully: “5 less than n” is n − 5; “twice a number increased by 3” is 2n + 3.</li><li>To <b>evaluate</b>, replace each variable with its value in parentheses, then follow GEMDAS: if x = −3, then x² = (−3)² = 9.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>3a − 2b when a = 4, b = −1</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3(4) − 2(−1)</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>12 + 2</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 14</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>3x as 3 + x</td><td>3x means 3 × x.</td></tr><tr><td>“5 less than n” as 5 − n</td><td>It is n − 5.</td></tr><tr><td>−3² for x²</td><td>Use (−3)² = 9.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does MATATAG Math 7 ask about expressions?",
   "a": "Distinguish a variable from a constant, evaluate algebraic expressions given the values of the variables, and translate verbal phrases into algebraic expressions."
